00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.400
So before getting into
the actual replicator,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.820
I want to spend some time
around the data infrastructure

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.810
we use for analytics.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.070
So at Twitter, we
primarily use Hadoop

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.410
for large scale analytics.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:13.370
Hadoop Distributed File System,
HDFS, is our primary storage.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.790
We have multiple clusters,
large clusters, which

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.510
can host up to exabyte of data.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.900
There are multiple
clusters per use cases.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.130
We have production
clusters, ad hoc clusters.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.350
And each of those clusters have
different properties and also

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.230
machine configuration,
based on the use case,

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:38.630
to make sure we always
optimize both the jobs as well

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.640
as the cluster utilization.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:48.040
So this like a diagram of
how the actual data flows

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.430
at a very high level.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.855
So whenever users
use twitter.com

00:01:52.855 --> 00:01:55.870
or use the Twitter
mobile, their actions

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:00.160
generate a lot of events,
what we call as events.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.420
We generate about 1.5
trillion events every day.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.180
That's about 1 billion
events every minute.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.360
So all these events flow through
a system called a log pipeline.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.580
So each of these events have
a name associated with that.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.530
So the log pipeline is
responsible for aggregating

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.130
all the events for the
same name and write them

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:30.140
as log files on HDFS.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.410
We call this as
a real time HDFS.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.680
This acts like a incoming
HDFS and usually does

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.880
a lot of cleansing,
format conversion,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.610
and then also does our
time ordered partition,

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:45.310
like [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:49.180
So all the events
which got generated

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.610
from thousands of
different end points

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.580
end up as log files for
that particular hour

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:55.330
in the same place.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.070
Right?

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.300
We call this real
time Hadoop cluster

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:03.040
is because this is the entry
point for our log files.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.600
The SLA on these
clusters are very high.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.100
This cluster been up for several
years for, as we always do,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.200
a rolling upgrade.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.160
From there, once the
data set gets collected

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.870
as a hourly partition, users
would have configured, OK,

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:24.340
which cluster that those
data has to be replicated.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.560
So it can be replicated to
production or ad hoc clusters,

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.280
based on the data set
and the configuration.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.510
And then once the data
lands on these clusters,

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.250
they run more jobs on that and
generate even more data sets.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:44.230
And those data sets
can also be configured

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.760
to be replicated to either a
cold storage, or production use

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.740
cases, and all those things.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.880
Apart from our batch
analytics, the log pipeline

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.660
will also stream some of
the events for our real time

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.500
computing.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:04.360
So we use Apache Heron for our
real time computing framework.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.150
So today, overall
in our clusters,

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.430
we crunch hundreds
of petabytes of data

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:15.580
everyday across thousands
of jobs turning every day.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.649
So when you look at one of the
cluster, what is in a cluster?

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:24.700
So in a cluster, you
will see many data sets

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.150
and the application services
responsible for replicating

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.690
that data from somewhere,
from source to this cluster.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.460
With every data
set, there is also

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.150
a retention associated with it.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.880
So we have a retention
service which

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.860
takes care of deleting the
data based on that data set's

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.190
configuration.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.740
We have multiple different
compute frameworks,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:49.540
like Scalding, Presto, Hive,
like MapReduce jobs, which

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.510
crunch the data
and do analytics.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.810
So they interact with
a system called DAL,

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.800
or Data Access Layer, which
is like our metadata service.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.790
So the DAL has information
of all the data

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:07.450
sets and the partitions,
hourly partitions,

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.270
associated with that data set.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:12.040
Whenever a replication
service copies a new data set,

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.140
the DAL is again updated.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.170
We also have a front
end for the DAL, which

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.860
we call the eagle
eye, which gives

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:25.420
a global overview of any
particular data set at Twitter.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:28.300
So our data set will
have a logical name,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.480
and it will have multiple
physical locations, which

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.780
means that same data
set is available

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.000
at multiple different clusters.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.710
And we'll also have
different properties

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.820
associated with them, like
schema, owner, what kind

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:48.280
of the data it has, like whether
it has PIA data or such, right?

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.410
It also has information
about whether this data

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:56.210
set is a hourly partition or
a daily partition and such.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:57.760
So there are some
more information

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.720
in some of the blogs about this.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:03.630
So I also want to touch upon--

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.250
we are like, now
talking about a lot

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.860
of data spread across many
different clusters, right?

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.660
How do we identify this?

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.010
So traditionally, people who
have been using Hadoop know

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:18.690
that, when there was one HDFS,
you used to refer to the data

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:24.600
inside of HDFS using hdfs://,
a name node name, and a port,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.880
and a path within that.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.890
Then came multiple
namespaces and HA.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.110
So with that, the
open source community

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.230
introduced the concept
of view file system.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:39.190
View file system is a
client side mount table

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.770
where the client
will resolve which

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.770
particular name node it has
to redirect the requests to.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.160
So this way, you could
scale multiple name nodes

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.230
and also add in HA
for the name nodes.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:56.350
So you would reference
something like viewfs://,

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:01.060
and then cluster-X, and
then the data within that.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.880
In this example, we're
following /logs/partly-cloudy.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.340
Partly cloudy is
also the project name

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.610
which we use for this effort.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.270
At Twitter, as I said,
we have multiple clusters

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:14.870
and multiple data centers.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:18.610
So what we ended up doing was
to extend that view file system

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:21.670
to add-- we call it something
called as a Twitter view file

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:22.450
system.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:23.890
With this, what
you can do is you

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:29.000
can globally identify our data,
which is stored in any cluster.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.817
So you would say /datacenter1,
cluster x, and then the data.

00:07:33.817 --> 00:07:36.400
And the client side mount table
will take care of resolving it

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.580
to the right Hadoop name node.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:43.120
So now what happens is that the
tools, such as a replicator,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.740
will use these globally
identifiable parts

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:54.190
and then act upon the data
which it's replicating, right?

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:55.930
So what is the need
for replication?

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:58.850
As I said, we have multiple
different clusters.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.550
This is just a snapshot.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.040
As you can see, we have
thousands of different data

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:05.230
sets people configure.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:08.620
And they need to be kept
in sync, like not just one

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.680
from source to destination, but
there is source, destination,

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:12.380
back.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.940
And then there is
also merging, right?

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.900
When you say replication,
at the very high level

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:21.410
we have two kinds
of replication.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:24.290
One is a source to destination.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:25.390
It's a one-to-one.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:29.387
We always make sure
that any new data which

00:08:29.387 --> 00:08:30.970
gets generated in
the source has to be

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:32.650
kept in sync with destination.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:35.470
And we could also have
configuration wherein

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:38.440
you could have multiple
sources, and all the data

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.059
belonging to that
particular add has

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:43.039
to be merged and made
available at the destination.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:46.820
So this is like a
copy plus merge.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:50.540
So when users set
up a replication,

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.790
so typically what
they do is they

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:55.430
provide a data set name,
like a partly-cloudy

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:56.780
as a data set name.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:00.740
They provide source
cluster and source path,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.680
destination cluster and
the destination path,

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.900
and some other properties
associated with that,

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:12.170
and then add it to a
metadata layer, such as DAL.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.810
Replicator would fetch
that entry from DAL

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.470
and basically translate it
to saying that, OK, data

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:21.110
set partly-cloudy-- and this is
my source globally identified

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:26.650
path and globally
identified destination.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:29.580
So what is replicator
actually doing?

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:34.460
So a replicator is like a
[INAUDIBLE] running daemon,

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.360
which we run it on
frameworks such as Mesos

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.360
so that, even if
the node goes down,

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.970
Mesos can actually,
automatically start

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.540
that replicator somewhere else.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.060
It's completely stateless.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:50.480
Whenever it stacks up,
it queries DAL and then

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.010
asks, so give me all
the configurations

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.490
which is responsible for a
particular destination cluster.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.730
So we run one replicator
per destination cluster.

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:04.150
So it is responsible
in making sure

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:06.700
that all the data sets
from different sources

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:10.270
are in sync at the
destination cluster.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:14.110
So after fetching those
entries, for each of this entry,

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:17.890
it will do a metadata lookup
from the source cluster

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.180
to destination cluster.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.550
Basically, it does
a directory listing

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:24.700
to make sure all the
partitions are in sync.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.130
If any new partitions
are available,

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:31.860
then it kicks off a MapReduce
job, called Hadoop Distcp job.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.000
And Distcp job is the
one which is actually

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.690
transferring the data.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.750
The red arrow shows
transferring the data.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.000
And then Distcp
job writes locally

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.790
at the destination cluster.

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:46.760
As you can see, the source path
is ClusterX/logs/partly-cloudy.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.040
And then there is [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:49.850
And the destination
would be transferred

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:53.830
to the globally
identified path here.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:58.430
In cases where we have to
merge multiple data sets--

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:03.400
this is for data sets, as I
said, in the very beginning.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.280
The incoming data lands
on [? RT ?] clusters.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.890
So we have that set up on
multiple different data

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:09.920
centers.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:15.130
So for a developer who
needs to access data

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.050
for a particular hour
across all our data centers,

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:21.242
we need to make sure that we
merge for that particular hour.

00:11:21.242 --> 00:11:23.200
So in this case, what
happens is the replicator

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:28.520
identifies that this data set
is of type multiple sources.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:32.200
So it will kick off one
Distcp job per source

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.200
to destination, independent.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:38.020
And once it is done, it
will merge all of them

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:42.700
into a temporary space, and
then does a final atomic rename.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:44.890
So once after this is
done, it will make a call

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:47.950
to DAL saying that this
particular data set

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:50.710
at the destination cluster
for that particular hour

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:51.940
is now available.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:55.930
So all the tools who have
been waiting on the new data

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.250
can automatically start
consuming the data.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:05.290
So when we decided to
migrate some of our workload

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:11.230
to GCP to unlock
new features on GCP,

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:16.060
we started looking what all data
needs to be replicated in GCP.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.030
And just like the
previous configuration,

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:21.670
this was even more complicated.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:26.000
The data was spread across
multiple different clusters,

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.210
and we had to replicate
all of them to GCP.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:36.040
And once it lands on GCP, we
could unlock a lot of tools,

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.180
such as Presto on GCP,
Scalding, Hadoop clusters

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:41.980
on GCP, BigQuery.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:44.140
All the data which gets
replicated with GCS

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:46.960
can be loaded into
BigQuery, and then unlock

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.640
all these different
possibilities.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.770
So what did we do?

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:55.660
We tried to see
how did we do this.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.745
So remember, I talked about the
View FileSystem, Twitter View

00:12:58.745 --> 00:12:59.590
FileSystem.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:06.340
So to access the data which
is stored in Google GCS,

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:09.640
which is a bucket in GCS,
you use something like this,

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:17.720
gs:// and then bucket
name, logs.partly-cloudy.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:23.320
So Google has open-sourced
this library called,

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:25.100
Cloud Storage Connector.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.020
What it essentially is--

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:32.980
Hadoop file system API is
like an abstract file system.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:36.970
And all the other file systems
can implement those interfaces.

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:39.340
Like HDFS is actually
an implementation

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:40.720
of that file system.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:44.080
So Google has implemented
the same interface

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:48.040
to provide the file
system abstraction

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.560
for the additional upper layers.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.140
So anybody who is using
the file system APIs

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:56.650
can transparently access
Google Cloud Storage

00:13:56.650 --> 00:14:00.010
as if they're using
some Hadoop command.

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:03.010
So all the Hadoop commands,
the jobs which you run,

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.020
everything will run as is.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:08.740
So we plugged this one
to our View FileSystem

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:09.830
as a mount point.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:17.050
So what we did was any path in
Twitter which refers to /gcs

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:22.900
will automatically
translate to buckets on GCS,

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.700
using the Google Cloud
Storage Connector.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:28.340
Right?

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.920
So let's look at what is
happening with the replicator.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:32.570
So for the most
part, we didn't have

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.630
to change too much
in replicator code

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:39.200
because it was using the
Hadoop file system APIs.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:43.820
But what we ended up doing was,
as of our other applicators,

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:48.510
we run a GCS replicator
per destination.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:52.760
We created something called
as a Copy Cluster, which

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:57.080
acts as a destination, and set
up a replicator for GCS alone.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:15:01.790
So this Copy Cluster has
all the GCS mount points,

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:02.870
as I described.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:07.400
So any data which gets returned
to /gcs is transparently

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:11.870
getting redirected to Google
Cloud Storage buckets.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:16.160
So when you're replicating an
input data set from a ClusterX

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:21.230
to a destination /gcs, what is
happening is the Distcp job is

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:24.620
transferring the data
from the source cluster.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.510
And using the Cloud
Storage Connector,

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:32.630
it's writing the data
directly to buckets on GCS.

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.930
So for this to
happen, we had to do

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:37.830
a few things for the network.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.530
So as I said, the
replicator does two things.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:42.380
One is the metadata
operation, which

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.680
is it compares the source
directories and the destination

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:46.640
directories.

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:51.530
So for that, we used a proxy
to reach the Google endpoints,

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:54.530
because the amount
of data which gets

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:57.420
transferred around metadata
operations is not that much.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.990
But the actual data is in
the order of petabytes.

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.540
So what our network
engineering team did

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:07.940
was to create this Twitter and
Google PNI, Private Network

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:11.900
Interconnect, so that all
the worker nodes in our Copy

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:15.170
Cluster have direct access
to the Google endpoints.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.360
So now, any jobs which
are running any task

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:21.230
Distcp jobs which are running
on Copy Cluster, whenever

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:23.000
it has to write some
data, it can directly

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.830
write to GCS for us.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:33.150
Apart from that, Google
also has this concept

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.340
of multiregion bucket.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.530
And while we were
doing this testing,

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:40.140
they introduced
dual-region buckets

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:44.010
wherein any data you
write into those buckets

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.600
are only available
in two regions.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.970
And from either of the
two regions, if you write,

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:51.090
it will be automatically
available to access

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.450
from the other region.

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:59.490
So our network engineering team
also created that PNI setup

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:00.523
for the data centers.

00:17:00.523 --> 00:17:02.190
We have multiple
different data centers.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.190
Whichever was closer
to the region,

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.540
they created that interface.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:10.859
So now for cases
of [INAUDIBLE] copy

00:17:10.859 --> 00:17:15.040
where we have multiple sources
writing into a destination,

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:17.460
we can set up Distcp
jobs independently.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.849
And all of them can
write into the same path

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:22.240
at the destination.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:26.910
So we also did this
because renames on HDFS

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:31.200
is pretty cheap, because
you have a name node.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:34.780
But on an object store,
it was pretty expensive.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.710
So for this particular
Distcp jobs alone,

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:40.890
it understands that
they are writing to GCS.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.500
So they try to write
in place, right?

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:48.560
So now, how do you get any
consistency in this case?

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:52.070
So in such cases,
what we did was--

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:54.830
this shows a state
diagram of how

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:58.160
two replicators with multiple
different sources writing

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.520
to a single destination
achieves consistency.

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:03.380
So each of the
replicator basically

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:06.890
kicks off it's part of the copy.

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:11.540
And all the files have a data
center name embedded in them.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:14.670
So there is no name
conflict, to begin with.

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:18.360
So once the copy is done, each
of the replicator will create

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:25.260
an _SUCCESS and source
data center name in there.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:29.200
So once it is done, each of them
independently will check if all

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:31.820
_SUCCESS have been found.

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:35.640
If they are found, they'll
make a call to DAL to say,

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:41.030
hey, the data set for the merge
data set is available on GCS.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:44.120
Even if there are multiple
updates to DAL, it is OK.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:46.640
So these are [INAUDIBLE]
important operations.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.990
So we are OK in such cases.

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:56.750
So with all this setup, how
does it look for our users?

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.970
One of the goal is to
make sure that we have

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:03.980
a uniform access for our users.

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:08.440
So EagleEye, as I said,
is a front-end interface

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.610
for our DAL.

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:12.230
Whenever we are
replicating to GCS,

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:17.360
we are also publishing
those partitions in DAL.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.410
So this EagleEye, if you
see for a particular data

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:25.310
set logs particularly, there are
multiple different locations,

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:30.440
physical locations,
dw2_hdfs, hive, and all.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:35.480
And now, GCS becomes just
another destination for them.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:39.740
So for them, the data is
in HDFS, as well as GCS.

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:41.780
And you also have a
graph, which shows

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:46.130
the latency of copying each
partition to GCS, just like how

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:52.030
we have for our HDFS clusters.

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:57.440
So DAL provides a lot of APIs to
identify for a particular data

00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:58.800
set what are all the partition.

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:01.080
It also has command-line tools.

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:04.930
Now if you were to query,
OK, show me a logical data

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:08.760
set named called partly-cloudy,
you get an entry.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:12.420
And when you actually look
at all the physical locations

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:17.070
for that, you can see there is
one is HDFS, and one is GCS.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:21.000
So it's the same interface,
same APIs for our users

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.520
who have been using DAL.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:26.700
And you can query
all the partitions

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:29.800
for that particular data set
at a particular location.

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.710
So it will give you
hourly partitions.

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:34.480
As and when replicator
is copying data,

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:36.450
it is also publishing
this information

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:40.710
to DAL, the uniform
interface for our users.

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:44.820
Not just users, like for
ourselves or our SREs.

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:48.510
So since it's just another
instance of replicator,

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:50.760
we use the same
monitoring tools.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:54.030
So in this case, we are showing
for a particular category what

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:57.600
is the read/write bytes and what
is the latency of replicating

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.260
from source to destination.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:02.490
So one of those colors is
basically just another cloud

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:05.560
replicator for us.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:09.600
We have also comprehensive
alerting for our existing

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:10.200
replicator.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:13.950
The same thing translated for
the Google replicator, as well.

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:15.910
We have one.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:19.080
And if there is some
problems, for example,

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:22.990
in the bottom graph, you see
some Distcp jobs are failing.

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:27.000
So we can configure rules
which will say pages

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.000
if you cross some threshold.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:38.120
So as part of organizing all
the resources in GCP, tomorrow

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:41.200
morning, there is an excellent
talk from our teammate,

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:44.840
[INAUDIBLE],, who's going to
talk more about how we organize

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:50.360
our projects, and resources,
and users into different GCP

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:51.140
projects.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.310
So you should
definitely check it out.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:57.290
So essentially, on GCP,
we have different projects

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:59.930
for different organization.

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:02.660
Now, we took advantage of
that and said, OK, we'll

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:07.210
deploy different
replicators per project.

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:09.350
And these replicators
are going to be

00:22:09.350 --> 00:22:12.710
using separate service
account credentials and all

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:13.760
those things.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:18.590
So all the data which is owned
by a particular organization,

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:21.290
that replicator will be
responsible for replicating

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:22.290
the data.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.200
So if you have
problems with the keys,

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:28.390
you just have to rotate the keys
for that particular replicator.

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:31.190
Or if you want to do
some updates, or testing,

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:34.070
or anything, so just
adding another more

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:38.540
replicator for that.

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:41.750
We also did one more thing.

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:43.920
As I said, in the
Twitter View FileSystem,

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.880
it's basically a client-side
mount table to say,

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:52.190
if you have two namespaces,
you would have two entries,

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:54.500
saying that anything which
starts with the prefix,

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:57.170
go to first entry.

00:22:57.170 --> 00:22:58.230
If not, second.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:02.030
But with GCS, what
happens is we'll

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:04.670
be managing thousands of
users, which translates

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:06.140
to thousands of buckets.

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:11.120
And also, you will have
hundreds of categories or data

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.860
sets, which translates to
hundreds of different buckets.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:18.020
So instead of creating hundreds
of entries in the client-side

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:21.770
mount table, what we ended up
doing is to implement a regular

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:27.950
expression-based client-side
mount table wherein the path,

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:33.410
such as /gcs/logs/partly-cloudy,
will translate

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:37.140
to gs:logx.partly-cloudy.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:41.420
We also are maintaining a
well-defined naming convention

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:42.690
for our bucket.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:45.200
So this makes it very
easy for us to do

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:48.407
this regex-based conversion.

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:54.800
So with all this setup,
where are we today?

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:57.410
We have tens of
different of instances

00:23:57.410 --> 00:24:00.960
of these replicators
per project, per test

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:04.920
setup across multiple data
centers, which are running.

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:08.810
We have copied petabytes,
tens of petabytes of data.

00:24:08.810 --> 00:24:11.420
Many use cases have been
unlocked, like BigQuery,

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:13.980
Presto, and GCP, and all.

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:19.010
And there will be lot of
blog posts coming soon

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:22.820
about all those things.

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:27.990
And so this effort was made
possible only with close

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.460
collaboration with
the folks at Google.

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:33.720
So while we were testing
this out and then doing

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:36.420
some iterations,
there were a lot

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:41.880
of enhancements for the
Google Cloud Hadoop connector.

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:45.450
For example, when
we started off,

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:50.790
HDFS has a different
checksum than GCS.

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:53.490
So if you run a Distcp
job, even though the data

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:56.550
is copied successfully,
it will check the checksum

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.400
and say, there is corruption.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:00.030
And then it will
rerun the job again.

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:03.070
It will delete everything
and then rerun the job again.

00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:05.040
So when we talked with
the Google engineers,

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:06.030
they identified this.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:09.120
And then they started to make
changes to the Hadoop code

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:14.340
itself to provide a flexible
checksum option, which

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:15.870
is now open-source.

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:17.830
There's also a blog
post about that.

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:19.470
And while that was
happening, we also

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:21.780
made modifications
to our Distcp jobs

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:26.700
to do a streaming checksum
until this was deployed.

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:28.980
There were various
other enhancements

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:33.060
of other replicators, such
as proxy configuration

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:38.370
as a path to a file, instead of
encoding user name and password

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:39.840
of a proxy credential.

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:41.610
Because what used to
happen is that used

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.820
to get loaded into
configuration object.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:47.040
And in Hadoopland, once it
is in configuration object,

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:51.030
it gets passed
around everywhere.

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:53.610
And also when we
started off, Google

00:25:53.610 --> 00:26:00.000
had only one way to specify
one user credentials.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.550
And then with that, we changed
it to per user credential.

00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:04.140
Whoever is running
the command, we

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:07.670
can dynamically pick up their
credentials to access GCS.

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:10.620
And when we were integrating
with our View FileSystem,

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:17.670
lazy initialization support
for the Google Cloud Storage.

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.980
Apart from that,
we have other teams

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:23.730
who are also evaluating a lot
of stuff on GCS, for example,

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:24.540
Presto.

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:28.140
So they discovered a lot of
performance improvements,

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:32.350
so which translated into
changes both at our end,

00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:35.490
open-source Presto
community, and also GCS.

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:37.410
So this was a very
good collaboration.

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:39.030
There'll be some blog posts.

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:45.810
And a lot of things around the
Google Cloud Storage Connector,

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:50.700
especially for the
object store, because we

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:52.950
have been used to using HDFS.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.590
And our applications
are used to thinking

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:59.520
in HDFS kind of way, wherein
renames, for example,

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:00.180
are atomic.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:02.310
But that is not possible on GCS.

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:07.080
So Google folks implemented this
cooperative locking wherein,

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:10.170
if you turn on that flag,
if multiple people are

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:13.410
trying to do a rename,
then it will take a log

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:14.540
and do a rename.

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:19.260
And then there is the FSCK for
recovery and all those things.

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:21.930
And in most of
the cases, what we

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:26.790
do is, whenever these
stuff are open-source,

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:31.040
we try to use the same
version, as much as possible.

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:35.790
That also makes it very easy for
us to report any support cases.

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:39.030
Whenever we file a
support case with Google,

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:41.640
we can just say the
version which we are using.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:43.650
And they can refer to
the open-source version

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:45.950
and say, OK, this is
what was happening.

00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:46.950
They can refer the code.

00:27:46.950 --> 00:27:48.270
So that makes it very easy.

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:57.170
In summary, I hope I was able
to explain the replicator

00:27:57.170 --> 00:28:01.580
architecture, while extending
it to the Google Cloud

00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:04.010
and maintaining the same
consistent interface

00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:06.110
for our users.

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:09.410
I also want to acknowledge
a few of our team members.

00:28:09.410 --> 00:28:13.610
A lot of people worked on many
different components of this.

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:18.230
And this special link,
cloud.google.com.twitter,

00:28:18.230 --> 00:28:21.260
where there'll be a lot
more information, and blog

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:24.930
posts, and linking,
cross-linking and such.

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:29.650
Finally, we have a lot of
openings across the board.

00:28:29.650 --> 00:28:33.750
And I encourage you to
tweet or apply online.

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:36.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

